let me begin by by saying something that  perhaps is not generally talked about in  business and it's a word that I use to  categorize one of the chapters in the  book it's not a word that generally is  used in business schools or in business  classes and it's love I've been at  Starbucks almost 30 years and I came  back to the company in January of o8  because of unbridled enthusiasm and love  and affection for the company and  200,000 people who wear the green apron  and my deep responsibilities to them and  their families to restore the company  back to its rightful place and other  than my family I can honestly say that  there isn't anything that I would not do  to defend this company and certainly  enhance its standing in the marketplace  Starbucks Coffee Company in a very  unique way not better than any other  company a set out to build a different  type of business model and that business  model was based on core values that  specifically was designed to achieve the  fragile balance between profitability  and a social conscience it's important  that you understand that the foundation  of the company was created in a very  unique way and and that uniqueness in in  in in a very in a way that is hard to  understand but I'll trying to explain it  is linked to me not because of my ego or  my strength of conviction but what I  experienced as a young child I grew up  in Brooklyn New York and for those of  you on the East Coast you would be  familiar with the term the projects for  those of you on the west coast it's  federally subsidized housing  and my dad never made more than $20,000  a year we were a blue-collar lower-class  family the rent when I was a kid that my  parents were paying was 96 dollars a  month for a one-bedroom apartment and it  was a tough childhood when I was 7 years  old I came home from school 1960 and I  walked in and my father was spread on a  couch with a cast from his waist to his  ankle he had a terrible he had a series  of really bad jaws but this one  particular job was probably the worst  he was a diaper delivery man before the  invention of pampers you can imagine and  he fell on a sheet of ice and broke his  hip and an ankle and in 1960 if you were  a blue-collar worker and you got hurt on  the job you were basically dismissed  there was no workmen's compensation  there was no health insurance and we  were just I was witnessing at the age of  7 literally the fracturing of the  American dream  and and I witnessed what my parents went  through in terms of my father's  self-esteem and just what it was like to  be left behind now I never imagined my  wildest dreams at a kid from Brooklyn on  the other side of the tracks would one  day be in a position to be part of a  great company or certainly be in a  position to build a great company but  once I was I realized that that  imprinting moment at the age of 7 in  1960 needed to be linked to something  for the future of our company and in a  way I wanted to try and build the kind  of company that my father never got a  chance to work for so almost 26 years  ago  Starbucks became the first company in  America to provide comprehensive health  insurance and equity in the form of  stock options to every single employee  including part-time workers that was a  huge huge commitment on our part and we  did that when we were a private company  still losing money fast forward that  benefit today will cost Starbucks this  year 256  million dollars our largest single line  item on our income statement other than  the price and other than green coffee  for the whole corporation
 

